---
// The Backend skill's showcase: a 6-step engineering pipeline, framed the
// way QA is — the coding agent is the junior who implements, the
// orchestrator is the senior who reviews and proves it. Not Cloud/DevOps's
// AI-compares-trade-offs framing: there's no trade-off comparison here,
// there's a fix and a test that has to fail on the old code first.
//
// Box text stays plain-English, same rule as every diagram since Cloud's
// revision — technical specifics (Redis, atomic ops, the engine facade)
// live one layer down in `details[].body`.
//
// Coordinates reused verbatim from AiWorkflowDiagram.astro: a linear 6-step
// pipeline, so the same 3x2 snake geometry (246-wide boxes, viewBox
// 820x400) applies without modification.
// Source facts: src/content/cards/backend.md, KB/docs/context/backend.md,
// KB/docs/context/testing.md.
---

<figure class="card-diagram">
  <div class="card-diagram-scroll">
    <svg
      class="topology"
      viewBox="0 0 820 400"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="backend-pipeline-title backend-pipeline-desc"
      xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
      fill="none"
    >
      <title id="backend-pipeline-title">Six steps from a hard requirement to an admitted gap</title>
      <desc id="backend-pipeline-desc">
        Six steps, in order. One: the tower-stability math is required to
        depend on nothing but the tower's current state — no history, no
        randomness — so it always grades the same tower the same way. Two:
        gameplay rules are split into focused pieces, but every call between
        them still goes back through one shared entry point. Three: a room
        follows its own player automatically if a different machine ends up
        answering their connection. Four: two machines picking players off
        the same waiting list can collide under real load, so the fix takes
        exactly what it needs in one indivisible step rather than reading
        then rewriting. Five: that fix is proven by a test that first runs
        the same scenario against the old, broken logic to confirm it
        actually fails there, then checks it against the fix. Six: what
        isn't built yet — a real persistent leaderboard — is stated
        plainly as future work, not left for someone else to discover.
      </desc>

      <defs>
        <marker id="backend-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path class="arrow-head" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" stroke="none" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="objectives" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 1, the same math every time — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="15" y="40" width="246" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="29" y="64">1 · The same math, every time</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="82">no history, no randomness</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="98">so every checker agrees</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M261,82 L287,82" marker-end="url(#backend-arrow)" />

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="architecture" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 2, one entrance not five — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="287" y="40" width="246" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="301" y="64">2 · One entrance, not five</text>
        <text class="sub" x="301" y="82">every call goes through it</text>
        <text class="sub" x="301" y="98">even between two pieces</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M533,82 L559,82" marker-end="url(#backend-arrow)" />

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="core_service" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 3, a room follows its player — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="559" y="40" width="246" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="573" y="64">3 · A room follows its player</text>
        <text class="sub" x="573" y="82">even to a different machine</text>
        <text class="sub" x="573" y="98">the player never notices</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M682,124 L682,230" marker-end="url(#backend-arrow)" />

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="performance" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 4, no two writes collide — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="559" y="230" width="246" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="573" y="254">4 · No two writes collide</text>
        <text class="sub" x="573" y="272">even under real concurrency</text>
        <text class="sub" x="573" y="288">not just in easy tests</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M559,272 L533,272" marker-end="url(#backend-arrow)" />

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="validation" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 5, proven to fail first then proven fixed — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="287" y="230" width="246" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="301" y="254">5 · Proven to fail, then fixed</text>
        <text class="sub" x="301" y="272">the old code, run first</text>
        <text class="sub" x="301" y="288">then checked that it fails</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M287,272 L261,272" marker-end="url(#backend-arrow)" />

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="production_readiness" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 6, said out loud not hidden — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="15" y="230" width="246" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="29" y="254">6 · Said out loud, not hidden</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="272">what's not built yet</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="288">and why that's fine for now</text>
      </g>

      <text class="wire accent" x="15" y="368">The agent wrote the atomic fix</text>
      <text class="wire" x="15" y="384">proving it worked meant running the old, broken code through the same test first</text>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="card-diagram-caption">
    Click any step to read what it means.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
